  "verbalization": "Sulindac is implicated in the pathogenesis of arthropathy through its association with osteoarthritis susceptibility, which in turn links to multiple arthropathic conditions. The evidence suggests that Sulindac may modulate pathways involved in joint inflammation and degeneration, as it is linked to indications for ketoprofen, felbinac, and choline magnesium trisalicylate, all of which are known to have anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties. These compounds are often used in the treatment of arthropathy, indicating a potential role for Sulindac in the broader context of arthropathic conditions. The shared indication of osteoarthritis susceptibility across these pathways suggests a possible mechanism by which Sulindac could contribute to the development of arthropathy, possibly through the modulation of inflammatory mediators or the promotion of joint degeneration. The integrated evidence highlights a potential link between Sulindac and arthropathy via its influence on osteoarthritis susceptibility, supported by the overlapping indications and the shared clinical relevance to arthropathic conditions.",
